      ":question/id": "a2c02942-de68-547a-8bfa-7932a2f390a1",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-103438",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/5b/5bbb0cacbe8dca2facfd49e757ba9b7aa40ee91ae820f001c72d637186d57b79.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement \u201c$f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\u201d is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=-1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{\u2032}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement \u201c$f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=0$\u201d is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=0,$ and therefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement \u201c$f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=1$\u201d is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=1$ since $f(1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true.",
