      ":question/problem": "![](images/99/991ced811f1c8f1e337025b6e2f7273d2b028a3af4a376efb8436956dc40fa62.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively. \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that \n\n- at $x=2$ there is a sharp corner, so ${f}^{\u2032}(2)$ doesn't exist, and\n\n- at $x=8$ there is a discontinuity, so  ${f}^{\u2032}(8)$ doesn't exist  either.\n\n![](images/cf/cf7b011f1cf09fecbdc60271ee17869b0260f7b2d3b03e26d8a675e38d1f2f7c.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two critical points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=2$ and $x=8.$"
