      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
      },
      ":question/id": "fc518e9d-35b1-5473-8374-94a56f67a024",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-48736",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/36/36477ec421ff4b7f349a060df762eadee83b008fbfc9e021a8893cc45e405cae.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's check our statements.\n\n- The statement \u201c$f(x)$ has  a stationary point at $x=2$\u201d is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=0.$\n\n- The statement \u201c$f(x)$ has  a local maximum at $x=-2$\u201d is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=-2$ since $f(-2)\\ge f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough  to $x=-2.$\n\n- The statement \u201c$f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-2$\u201d is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a sharp edge at $x=-2$ and therefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(-2)$ does not exist.\n\nTherefore, select \u201c$f(x)$ has  a stationary point at $x=2$\u201d and \u201c$f(x)$ has  a local maximum at $x=-2$.\u201d"
    }
  },
